\p{Given a group #{G}, a subgroup #{H} of #{G} and the set of left cosets of #{H} in #{G}, #{(G : H)}, we would like to define a group operation #{\circ} on the cosets so that #{((G : H), \circ)} is a group. We would like}

##{(gH) \circ (kH) = gkH.}

\p{When does this work? Consider #{gHkH}; if #{Hk = kH} then #{gHkH = gkHH = gkH}. This motivates the following definition.}

